{
  "schema": "firstcall.multi002.stripe.eligibility",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "assessment_timestamp_utc": "2026-09-18T00:00:00Z",
  "assessor": "Claude Code (Opus 4.8) takeover of interrupted Codex documentary eligibility gate",
  "repository_commit": "e9be04017b52df81f65689e537f6e49b3a1d119c",
  "design_document": "docs/multi002-experiment-design.md",
  "design_sha256": "3c3e867d15ae7c74ee7f70cee5c0e769697012afcc54ce6f80bf64cc23179f99",
  "decision": "NOT_ELIGIBLE",
  "decision_reasons": [
    "R1 (handoff not vendor-supported): Stripe's own onboarding treats environment variables as the store for API KEYS only. The quickstart section 'Set your environment variables' (S06) instructs developers to place the publishable and secret keys (and optionally a webhook secret) into a .env file. No captured official Stripe source defines, recommends, or uses STRIPE_PRICE_ID or any price-identifier environment variable as an operational/deployment handoff.",
    "R2 (price supplied inline, not via env): In every official Checkout code sample the Price identifier appears as an inline template placeholder {{PRICE_ID}} accompanied by the instruction 'Provide the exact Price ID (for example, price_1234) of the product you want to sell' (S06 six occurrences across JS/Ruby/Python/PHP/.NET/Go; S10 curl). Stripe's convention is a literal value the developer looks up and pastes inline, not a handed-off environment variable.",
    "R3 (unambiguous machine-discovery route from the named product): The frozen customer task names the catalog product ('the existing FIRSTCALL Test Plan catalog price'). With the valid test secret key (named in the task) and the read-only exploration the task explicitly permits, the intended Price is reachable by documented, permitted routes: Search products by name (S34 POST /v1/products/search) then List/Search prices filtered to that product (S20 GET /v1/prices with documented `product` parameter: 'Only return prices for the given product'; S35 POST /v1/prices/search), or List prices directly (S20). This is an explicit, unambiguous route from task to intended price, which the design gate (Sec 3 'Disqualifying alternative'; Sec 4 item 5) states disqualifies the candidate.",
    "R4 (Stripe's own deployment-stable price reference is lookup_key, not an env var): Stripe documents lookup_key precisely for the deployment-handoff scenario the experiment invents (S28: prior practice 'is often manual and requires you to deploy new code. To better manage these scenarios, you can use the lookup_key attribute'; retrievable via S20 lookup_keys param, S33 price object, S35). The authentic vendor-supported stable handoff is a Price lookup_key, not STRIPE_PRICE_ID.",
    "R5 (agent-native live discovery exists): Stripe ships agent-native discovery that resolves account catalog objects without a human-provided ID: Agent skills, Agent plugins, `stripe agent setup`, and a Stripe MCP server that plugins 'automatically configure' to expose live account data, plus terminal-readable docs via `stripe docs` (S43, echoed as the 'Start here' banner on S34/S35). A fresh autonomous agent has documented, first-class routes to enumerate the account's real Prices.",
    "CONCLUSION: Withholding knowledge of STRIPE_PRICE_ID does not reveal a genuine machine-discoverability defect. It would manufacture the baseline failure by depending on the agent NOT exercising the documented, permitted catalog read/search/MCP routes that lead directly to the task-named Price. The hidden STRIPE_PRICE_ID handoff is researcher-created rather than vendor-supported. Both independent grounds (R1-R2 and R3-R5) require rejection under the pre-registered gate."
  ],
